Acid/Base Balance (Start times: 30:00)\
In order to solve acid-base disorders, it is\
important to know the normal values for\
pH, CO2 and HCO3 (bicarbonate), which\
are shown below\
\'95 pH 7.35 to 7.45\
\'95 CO2 35 to 45\
\'95 HCO3 22 to 26\
The first value to look at in an acid-base\
disorder is the pH\
\'95 If pH is <7.35, the acid-base imbalance\
is acidotic\
\'95 If pH is <7.45, the acid-base imbalance\
is alkalotic\
Now, to determine if the imbalance is metabolic or respiratory, determine whether HCO3 goes\
in the same or opposite direction with pH\
\'95 Rule of the Bs: If pH and Bicarb move both in the same direction, then the acid-base\
imbalance is metabolic \'85 Otherwise, it is respiratory\
Example #1\
\'95 pH 7.3 Acidotic\
\'95 HCO3 20 Metabolic\
\'95 This is an example of metabolic acidosis\
Example #2\
\'95 pH 7.58 Alkalotic\
\'95 HCO3 32 Metabolic\
\'95 This is an example of metabolic alkalosis\
Example #3\
\'95 pH 7.22 Acidosis\
\'95 HCO3 35 Respiratory\
\'95 This is an example of respiratory acidosis\
As the pH goes, so goes my patient, except for Potassium \'85 That means\
\'95 If pH is low, everything is low, except potassium\
\'95 If pH is high, everything is high, except potassium\
Lecture 1 \'95 Mark Klimek \'95 92:21\
